        <div id="impact-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Impact Whey Protein: The Label in Detail
          </h2>

          <p>
            Impact Whey Protein is a whey concentrate. Depending on flavor and market, a 25 g scoop delivers roughly <strong>20 to 22 g of protein</strong> and about 90 to 105 calories, per label figures and nutrition databases at the time of writing. Unflavored versions sit at the top of that range because there is no flavoring or cocoa diluting the protein; dessert flavors sit lower. That is a protein content of roughly 80 to 88 percent by weight, which is normal for a concentrate.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Impact Whey Protein, typical 25 g scoop</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">20-22 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein (varies by flavor)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">90-105</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">30 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Powder for about 25 g protein</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">30,000+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Reviews on Myprotein's site</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The number that matters is the scoop math. US brands typically size the serving to hit 24 or 25 g of protein, so their scoops are 30 to 32 g. Myprotein sizes Impact Whey at 25 g of powder. To get 25 g of protein, you need roughly 29 to 31 g of Impact Whey, about one and a fifth of its own scoops. That means a bag that says 40 servings gives you closer to 33 servings at 25 g of protein each.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of that is a trick, and the label is clear about it. It simply means you should compare cost per 25 g of protein, not cost per serving. On that basis Impact Whey is still frequently one of the cheaper whey powders you can buy, especially in larger bags during a sale, but the gap with US brands is smaller than the price per bag suggests.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavor range is the brand's other big strength. Myprotein offers dozens of Impact Whey flavors, from basics like chocolate and vanilla to limited-edition dessert and licensed flavors, plus an unflavored version for smoothies and cooking. Buyer feedback on taste varies widely by flavor, so reading recent reviews for the exact flavor you want is more useful than reading reviews of the product as a whole.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="isolate-vs-concentrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Impact Whey vs Impact Whey Isolate
          </h2>

          <p>
            Impact Whey Isolate is filtered further to remove more of the lactose, fat and carbohydrate. In the UK, a 25 g scoop lists about 20 to 22 g of protein and roughly 90 calories. The US version is sized differently: nutrition databases list about 25 g of protein and around 110 calories per scoop of roughly 29 to 31 g, and the Amazon US listing we checked carries 25g in its title. Either way, the protein share per gram is higher than the concentrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical differences are digestion and texture. Isolate carries much less lactose, so people who get bloated on regular whey often do better with it, although it is not lactose-free and is not suitable for a milk allergy. It also mixes thinner and lighter, which some people prefer in water and others find less satisfying than a creamier concentrate. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> guide covers the trade-off in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cost is the deciding factor for most buyers. Isolate is more expensive to make, so Impact Whey Isolate usually costs more per 25 g of protein than Impact Whey. If you tolerate lactose fine, the concentrate gives you more protein for the money. If you do not, the isolate is worth paying for, and on Amazon US it is the easier product to buy anyway.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a third option in the range, Myprotein's clear whey, which is also an isolate but processed into a thin, juice-like drink rather than a shake. It solves a different problem, the heaviness of milky shakes in summer or after cardio, and is covered in its own section below.
          </p>

