        <div id="clear-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Clear Whey and the Vimto Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clear whey is whey isolate that has been hydrolyzed and acidified so it mixes into a translucent, juice-style drink instead of a milky shake. Myprotein is one of the best-known brands in the format. The US Clear Whey Isolate listing on Amazon is a 35-serving, roughly 1.9 lb tub in flavors such as Lemonade, with about 20 g of protein per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            The <strong>Vimto</strong> version is the one many UK readers are searching for. It is a licensed flavor based on the British fruit cordial, sold through Myprotein and UK retailers including Sainsbury's, Morrisons, Boots and Costco UK. Per UK retailer listings, a roughly 26 g serving provides about 20 g of protein and around 89 calories, with under 2 g of carbohydrate and under 1 g of sugar, sweetened with sucralose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vimto Clear Whey is essentially a UK product. Some specialist import shops sell it to US buyers, but at a mark-up, and we could not find a brand-sold Vimto listing on Amazon US. If you are in the US and want a fruit-flavored clear whey, the US Clear Whey Isolate flavors are the practical choice, and our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide compares them with rivals from other brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear whey has trade-offs. It costs more per gram of protein than a standard isolate, it can foam when shaken, and the acidity and sweetness are not to everyone's taste. Letting it settle for a minute after shaking helps with the foam. It is best used as a refreshing post-workout drink rather than a meal replacement, because it has almost no fat or carbohydrate to keep you full.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cost per 25 g and the Discount-Code Game
          </h2>

          <p>
            Myprotein's pricing works differently from most US brands. The listed price on its own site is rarely what anyone pays; site-wide codes of a third off or more appear so often that the full price functions as a reference point rather than a real price. That means the only fair comparison is the sale price you can actually get, converted to cost per 25 g of protein.
          </p>

          <p>
            To do that conversion, divide the price you will pay by the number of servings, then multiply by 25 and divide by the protein per serving. For Impact Whey at about 21 g per scoop, the multiplier is roughly 1.2. In other words, a scoop that costs 80 cents is closer to 95 cents per 25 g of protein. Doing this once for each brand you are considering takes two minutes and removes most of the marketing from the decision.
          </p>

