        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Testing, Certifications and Label Trust
          </h2>

          <p>
            Some Myprotein products carry the Informed Choice mark. UK retail listings for Impact Whey Isolate, for example, show it, and the brand promotes the program on several products. Coverage varies by product, flavor and market, so if you are a drug-tested athlete, search the exact product in the Informed Choice or Informed Sport database rather than assuming a certificate on one tub applies to every other.
          </p>

          <p>
            For non-athletes, the more important question is whether the protein figure is honest. Whey concentrate and isolate figures in the 80 to 90 percent range by weight are consistent with real whey rather than amino-spiked blends, and Myprotein's labels list whey as the protein source rather than a blend padded with added glycine or taurine. That is the main thing to check on any budget brand.
          </p>

          <p>
            We could not find batch-level heavy-metal reports published by Myprotein, which a handful of premium US brands do provide. Whey generally tests far lower for heavy metals than plant proteins, which is part of why this is less of a concern for a whey brand than for a pea or rice powder. If heavy metals are a priority for you, our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">is protein powder safe</a> guide explains what independent testing has found across the category.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more trust point: on Amazon, check the seller. The Impact Whey Isolate listing we checked is sold by Myprotein North America and ships from Amazon. Listings sold by unfamiliar third parties, especially for UK-only flavors, are more likely to carry old stock or imported tubs with a different label, so stick to brand-sold listings where you can.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-us-brands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Myprotein vs Optimum Nutrition and Dymatize
          </h2>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey</strong>, Myprotein Impact Whey is usually cheaper per 25 g on sale, but Gold Standard delivers about 24 g of protein in a roughly 30 to 31 g scoop for about 120 calories per the label, is widely available on Amazon in many sizes, and is promoted as Informed Choice certified. For a US buyer who wants one tub without hunting for codes, Gold Standard is the simpler buy. Our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition review</a> covers it in depth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Against <strong>Dymatize ISO100</strong>, Myprotein's isolate is usually the cheaper of the two, while ISO100 offers 25 g of protein per scoop from hydrolyzed and regular isolate, a very thin mix and wide availability. If you are lactose sensitive and want the most predictable isolate, ISO100 is the benchmark; if you want a cheaper isolate and are happy with Myprotein's flavors, Impact Whey Isolate is a reasonable alternative. See our <a href="/dymatize-protein-powder-review/">Dymatize review</a> for the full range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where Myprotein clearly wins is flavor variety and format choice. Few brands offer as many flavors, an unflavored option, clear whey and isolate under one roof, and the frequent sales make it easy to try several. Where it loses is predictability: the scoop size, the code-driven pricing and the differences between markets all make it harder to compare at a glance.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are outside the US and UK, regional differences can be large. In India, for example, Myprotein sells regional flavors and competes with local brands such as MuscleBlaze, which we cover in our <a href="/muscleblaze-protein-review/">MuscleBlaze protein review</a>. Always read the local label, because protein per scoop can differ from what US or UK reviews describe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Skip Myprotein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip the concentrate if you are <strong>lactose sensitive</strong>. Impact Whey Protein carries more lactose than an isolate, and if regular whey leaves you bloated, a cheaper price will not make up for it. Move up to Impact Whey Isolate or another isolate instead, and talk to a doctor if your symptoms are more than mild.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you want <strong>one simple purchase</strong>. If you dislike waiting for sales, comparing codes and checking whether a flavor is available in your country, a US brand stocked in many sizes on Amazon will save you time. The value advantage of Myprotein shrinks a lot when you pay anything close to list price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it if you <strong>need a guaranteed certification</strong> on the exact product and flavor. Some Myprotein products are Informed Choice listed, but coverage varies. Tested athletes should either confirm the specific tub in the database or choose a brand whose whole range is NSF Certified for Sport.
          </p>

          <p>
            And as with any protein powder, check with a doctor or registered dietitian before using it if you have kidney disease, are pregnant or breastfeeding, take medication that interacts with diet, or have a milk allergy. Whey isolate is low in lactose but is still milk protein. This review is general information, not medical advice.
          </p>
